{
  "title": "SLOs for agentic features: beyond latency",
  "content": "The first time building postmortems fails in production, it rarely fails in the way any postmortem template expects. It's a one-line fix once you know where to look, and expensive until then. Here's what holds up in practice, and why.\n\n## The problem this solves\n\nAgentic features break in ways that traditional SLOs never catch. A tool-calling agent can return a 200 OK in 300ms, with zero errors logged, and still be completely wrong — it picked the wrong tool, hallucinated a parameter, or looped three times before giving up. Error rate and latency dashboards stay green while users quietly lose trust. This post is a step-by-step guide to defining, implementing, and alerting on SLOs that actually measure whether the agent did its job, using OpenTelemetry and Prometheus. By the end you'll have a working SLI pipeline that tracks task success, tool-call accuracy, and loop termination — the three signals that matter most for agents.\n\n## Prerequisites and what you'll build\n\nYou need:\n\n- A working agent loop (any framework — LangChain, LlamaIndex, or hand-rolled) that emits structured logs or spans.\n- OpenTelemetry SDK for your language. Python and Go are the most common in this space; examples below use Python.\n- A Prometheus-compatible metrics backend (Prometheus itself, Grafana Cloud, or VictoriaMetrics).\n- Basic familiarity with SLO concepts: SLI (indicator), SLO (objective), error budget.\n\nWhat you'll build:\n\n1. Three custom SLIs: **task success rate**, **tool-call validity rate**, and **loop termination rate**.\n2. A recording rule set that turns raw spans into SLO-compliant time series.\n3. Alerting rules that fire on error-budget burn, not on raw error counts.\n4. A minimal test harness that validates the SLI logic against synthetic traces.\n\nThis is not a theoretical exercise. Every step below maps to a metric you can query in Grafana within an hour.\n\n## Step 1 — Define what \"success\" means for your agent\n\nBefore writing any code, you need a binary outcome per agent invocation. That's the hardest part, and it's where most teams stall.\n\nFor a customer-support agent, success might be: the ticket was resolved without escalation AND the user didn't re-open it within 24 hours. For a code-generation agent, success might be: the generated patch compiles AND passes the existing test suite. For a research agent, success might be: the final answer cites at least one source from the allowed domain list.\n\nWrite this down as a function. It doesn't have to be perfect — it has to be consistent. A common trap is defining success too broadly (\"user seemed happy\") or too narrowly (\"exact string match on expected output\"). Both lead to SLIs that either never fire or fire constantly, and neither is useful.\n\nHere's a workable definition for a tool-using agent:\n\n```python\n# sli_definitions.py\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\nfrom typing import Any\n\n@dataclass\nclass AgentOutcome:\n    task_id: str\n    success: bool\n    tool_calls_valid: bool\n    terminated_cleanly: bool\n    error: str | None = None\n\ndef evaluate_outcome(trace: dict[str, Any]) -> AgentOutcome:\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a completed agent trace, return a binary outcome.\n    This is the single source of truth for all SLIs.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Success: the agent produced a final answer AND no tool raised an error\n    # that the agent failed to recover from.\n    final_answer = trace.get(\"final_answer\")\n    tool_errors = [\n        span for span in trace[\"spans\"]\n        if span[\"name\"].startswith(\"tool.\") and span.get(\"error\")\n    ]\n    recovered = all(\n        any(s[\"name\"] == \"llm.retry\" for s in trace[\"spans\"][i+1:])\n        for i, s in enumerate(trace[\"spans\"])\n        if s in tool_errors\n    )\n    success = bool(final_answer) and (not tool_errors or recovered)\n\n    # Tool-call validity: every tool call had a schema-valid argument set.\n    tool_calls = [s for s in trace[\"spans\"] if s[\"name\"].startswith(\"tool.\")]\n    tool_calls_valid = all(\n        s.get(\"attributes\", {}).get(\"args_valid\", False)\n        for s in tool_calls\n    )\n\n    # Termination: the agent stopped because it decided to, not because\n    # it hit a max-iteration cap or a timeout.\n    terminated_cleanly = trace.get(\"termination_reason\") == \"agent_finished\"\n\n    return AgentOutcome(\n        task_id=trace[\"task_id\"],\n        success=success,\n        tool_calls_valid=tool_calls_valid,\n        terminated_cleanly=terminated_cleanly,\n        error=trace.get(\"error\"),\n    )\n```\n\nThis function is the contract. Every SLI below derives from it. If you change what success means, you change it here, and the rest of the pipeline follows.\n\n## Step 2 — Instrument the agent loop with OpenTelemetry\n\nYou can't compute SLIs from logs alone — you need structured spans with the attributes your `evaluate_outcome` function reads. OpenTelemetry is the right tool because it's vendor-neutral and already has SDKs for every language you're likely using.\n\nThe key decision: emit one span per agent invocation (the root span), one child span per LLM call, and one child span per tool call. That gives you the tree structure needed to compute recovery and termination.\n\n```python\n# agent_instrumentation.py\nfrom opentelemetry import trace\nfrom opentelemetry.trace import Status, StatusCode\n\n\ntracer = trace.get_tracer(\"agent.runtime\")\n\n\ndef run_agent(task_id: str, user_input: str, tools: dict) -> str:\n    with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"agent.invoke\") as root:\n        root.set_attribute(\"task.id\", task_id)\n        root.set_attribute(\"agent.tool_count\", len(tools))\n\n        messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user_input}]\n        max_iterations = 10\n        termination_reason = \"max_iterations\"\n\n        for iteration in range(max_iterations):\n            with tracer.start_as_current_span(\"llm.call\") as llm_span:\n                llm_span.set_attribute(\"llm.iteration\", iteration)\n                response = call_llm(messages)\n                llm_span.set_attribute(\"llm.stop_reason\", response.stop_reason)\n\n            if response.stop_reason == \"end_turn\":\n                termination_reason = \"agent_finished\"\n                root.set_attribute(\"termination_reason\", termination_reason)\n                root.set_attribute(\"final_answer\", response.content)\n                return response.content\n\n            if response.tool_call:\n                tool_name = response.tool_call.name\n                with tracer.start_as_current_span(f\"tool.{tool_name}\") as tool_span:\n                    try:\n                        args = validate_args(tool_name, response.tool_call.arguments)\n                        tool_span.set_attribute(\"args_valid\", True)\n                        result = tools[tool_name](**args)\n                        tool_span.set_status(Status(StatusCode.OK))\n                    except Exception as exc:\n                        tool_span.set_attribute(\"args_valid\", False)\n                        tool_span.record_exception(exc)\n                        tool_span.set_status(Status(StatusCode.ERROR))\n                        result = f\"Error: {exc}\"\n                messages.append({\"role\": \"tool\", \"content\": result})\n\n        root.set_attribute(\"termination_reason\", termination_reason)\n        root.set_status(Status(StatusCode.ERROR, \"max_iterations\"))\n        return \"\"\n```\n\nTwo things to notice. First, `args_valid` is set on the tool span itself — that's what makes the tool-call-validity SLI computable without re-parsing logs. Second, `termination_reason` is set on the root span, which is what lets you distinguish \"agent finished\" from \"agent gave up.\"\n\nA common trap: teams instrument only the LLM call and forget the tool spans. Then they can measure latency and token usage but not whether the agent actually used tools correctly. If you're using a framework that auto-instruments LLM calls, check whether it also emits tool spans — many do not, and you'll need to add them manually.\n\n## Step 3 — Convert spans to SLIs with a collector processor\n\nRaw spans are not metrics. You need to aggregate them into counters that Prometheus can scrape. The OpenTelemetry Collector has a `spanmetrics` connector that does this for standard dimensions (latency, error status), but it doesn't know about `args_valid` or `termination_reason`. You have two options:\n\n1. Use the Collector's `transform` processor to rewrite span attributes into metric labels, then use `spanmetrics`.\n2. Emit metrics directly from your application alongside the spans.\n\nFor agentic SLIs, option 2 is usually simpler because the outcome logic lives in your code, not in the Collector config. Here's the pattern:\n\n```python\n# sli_metrics.py\nfrom opentelemetry import metrics\n\nmeter = metrics.get_meter(\"agent.sli\")\n\ntask_success = meter.create_counter(\n    \"agent.task.success.total\",\n    description=\"Count of agent tasks that succeeded\",\n)\ntask_failure = meter.create_counter(\n    \"agent.task.failure.total\",\n    description=\"Count of agent tasks that failed\",\n)\ntool_valid = meter.create_counter(\n    \"agent.tool.call.valid.total\",\n    description=\"Tool calls with schema-valid arguments\",\n)\ntool_invalid = meter.create_counter(\n    \"agent.tool.call.invalid.total\",\n    description=\"Tool calls with schema-invalid arguments\",\n)\nterminated_clean = meter.create_counter(\n    \"agent.termination.clean.total\",\n    description=\"Agent runs that terminated by decision\",\n)\nterminated_forced = meter.create_counter(\n    \"agent.termination.forced.total\",\n    description=\"Agent runs that hit max iterations or timeout\",\n)\n\n\ndef record_outcome(outcome):\n    attrs = {\"agent.name\": \"support_agent\"}\n    if outcome.success:\n        task_success.add(1, attrs)\n    else:\n        task_failure.add(1, attrs)\n    if outcome.tool_calls_valid:\n        tool_valid.add(1, attrs)\n    else:\n        tool_invalid.add(1, attrs)\n    if outcome.terminated_cleanly:\n        terminated_clean.add(1, attrs)\n    else:\n        terminated_forced.add(1, attrs)\n```\n\nCall `record_outcome` after every agent invocation. The counters are cheap; the aggregation happens at scrape time in Prometheus.\n\n## Step 4 — Define SLOs and error budgets\n\nNow you have time series. The SLO definition is a ratio over a rolling window. For a 30-day window at 99% success:\n\n```promql\n# SLI: task success rate over 30 days\nsum(rate(agent_task_success_total[30d]))\n/\nsum(rate(agent_task_success_total[30d]) + rate(agent_task_failure_total[30d]))\n```\n\n```promql\n# Error budget remaining (as a fraction of allowed failures)\n1 - (\n  (1 - (\n    sum(rate(agent_task_success_total[30d]))\n    /\n    sum(rate(agent_task_success_total[30d]) + rate(agent_task_failure_total[30d]))\n  )) / 0.01\n)\n```\n\nFor tool-call validity, the SLO should be tighter — invalid arguments are a bug in your schema or prompt, not a user-facing failure, and they're more actionable. A 99.9% target is reasonable. For loop termination, the target depends on your max-iteration setting; if you set it to 10 and the agent needs 12, that's a design problem, not a runtime problem.\n\n| SLI | Typical SLO | Why this target |\n|-----|-------------|-----------------|\n| Task success | 99% | User-facing; failures are visible |\n| Tool-call validity | 99.9% | Schema/prompt bug; should be near-zero failure |\n| Clean termination | 95% | Some forced terminations are acceptable for hard tasks |\n\nThese are starting points, not gospel. The important thing is that each SLI has a target and a window, and that the error budget is computed from the same window.\n\n## Step 5 — Alert on burn rate, not on raw failures\n\nA single failed agent run is not an incident. A sustained burn of your error budget is. The standard pattern is multi-window burn-rate alerting, which fires when the budget is being consumed faster than the SLO allows.\n\n```yaml\n# prometheus_rules.yml\ngroups:\n  - name: agent_slo\n    rules:\n      - record: agent:task_success_rate:30d\n        expr: |\n          sum(rate(agent_task_success_total[30d]))\n          /\n          sum(rate(agent_task_success_total[30d]) + rate(agent_task_failure_total[30d]))\n\n      - alert: AgentTaskSuccessBudgetBurnFast\n        expr: |\n          (1 - agent:task_success_rate:30d) > (14.4 * 0.01)\n        for: 5m\n        labels:\n          severity: critical\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"Agent task success budget burning 14.4x faster than allowed\"\n\n      - alert: AgentTaskSuccessBudgetBurnSlow\n        expr: |\n          (1 - agent:task_success_rate:30d) > (6 * 0.01)\n        for: 30m\n        labels:\n          severity: warning\n        annotations:\n          summary: \"Agent task success budget burning 6x faster than allowed\"\n```\n\nThe 14.4 and 6 multipliers come from the standard Google SRE workbook burn-rate math: for a 30-day window, a 1-hour burn at 14.4x consumes 2% of the budget, and a 6-hour burn at 6x consumes 5%. The exact numbers depend on your window and tolerance; the point is that you alert on the rate of budget consumption, not on individual failures.\n\n## How to verify it works\n\nYou can't wait for a real incident to know if your SLOs are wired correctly. Build a synthetic trace generator that produces known-good and known-bad outcomes, then assert that the metrics move as expected.\n\n```python\n# test_sli_pipeline.py\nfrom sli_definitions import evaluate_outcome\nfrom sli_metrics import record_outcome\n\n\ndef test_success_path():\n    trace = {\n        \"task_id\": \"t1\",\n        \"final_answer\": \"done\",\n        \"termination_reason\": \"agent_finished\",\n        \"spans\": [\n            {\"name\": \"llm.call\", \"attributes\": {}},\n            {\"name\": \"tool.search\", \"attributes\": {\"args_valid\": True}},\n        ],\n    }\n    outcome = evaluate_outcome(trace)\n    assert outcome.success is True\n    assert outcome.terminated_cleanly is True\n    assert outcome.tool_calls_valid is True\n\n\ndef test_invalid_tool_args():\n    trace = {\n        \"task_id\": \"t2\",\n        \"final_answer\": \"done\",\n        \"termination_reason\": \"agent_finished\",\n        \"spans\": [\n            {\"name\": \"tool.search\", \"attributes\": {\"args_valid\": False}},\n        ],\n    }\n    outcome = evaluate_outcome(trace)\n    assert outcome.tool_calls_valid is False\n    assert outcome.success is True  # agent recovered\n\n\ndef test_forced_termination():\n    trace = {\n        \"task_id\": \"t3\",\n        \"final_answer\": \"\",\n        \"termination_reason\": \"max_iterations\",\n        \"spans\": [],\n    }\n    outcome = evaluate_outcome(trace)\n    assert outcome.terminated_cleanly is False\n    assert outcome.success is False\n```\n\nRun these tests in CI. If someone changes the outcome logic and breaks the SLI contract, the tests fail before the change ships.\n\n## Common questions and variations\n\n**How do I handle agents that legitimately take multiple attempts?**\n\nDefine success at the task level, not the attempt level. A task that succeeds on the third retry is still a success. Count retries as a separate SLI (retry rate) if you want visibility into efficiency, but don't let retries pollute the success metric.\n\n**What about non-deterministic outputs where \"correct\" is subjective?**\n\nUse a rubric-based evaluator as a span attribute. Run the evaluator as a separate step (it can be an LLM call with a fixed prompt) and record its verdict as `eval.passed`. The SLI then becomes the rate of `eval.passed`. This adds cost, so sample — evaluate 10% of runs and extrapolate, or evaluate all runs for a week to calibrate, then sample.\n\n**Should I use spanmetrics or custom counters?**\n\nCustom counters if your outcome logic depends on multiple spans (which it usually does for agents). Spanmetrics is fine for per-span latency and error rate, but it can't express \"this tool call was valid AND the agent recovered from the error.\" That's a cross-span judgment, and it belongs in application code.\n\n**How does this work with managed observability platforms?**\n\nOpenTelemetry is the portability layer. If you emit OTLP, you can point at any backend that speaks it. The SLI math is the same; only the query language changes. The risk is vendor-specific features creeping into your alerting rules — keep the recording rules in PromQL if you can, so you can move backends without rewriting alerts.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\nStart by picking one agent feature and writing its `evaluate_outcome` function today. Don't try to cover every agent in your system at once — pick the one with the most user-visible failures, define success in a single Python function, and instrument it with the three counters above. Then open `prometheus_rules.yml` and add the burn-rate alert. That's a 30-minute change that will tell you more about your agent's real reliability than any latency dashboard ever will.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
